      -> %a = OpFMul %float %x %ucas_float_3eaaaaab
      (divide by a constant as a multiply; -2)
      proof: within the original's Vulkan precision (derived), not bit-exact
        k = fl(1/3) = 0.33333334; k*d = 1 + delta with delta = 2.98e-08 (k*d is exact in double)
          x*k = (x/d)(1 + delta), so |fl(x*k) - x/d| <= ulp/2 + |x/d| |delta| < (0.5 + 2^24 |delta|) ulp = 1 ulp (|v| < 2^24 ulp(v); subnormal results alike)
          x/d correctly rounded is within 0.5 ulp, and both are floats: they differ by at most 1 ulp
          Vulkan OpFDiv: 2.5 ulp (for |y| in [2^-126, 2^126]); the multiply is correctly rounded: 1 <= 2.5, so it is within the precision the division already had
        not bit-exact: x = 3.0000005: / 3 gives 1.0000001, * 0.33333334 gives 1.0000002 (the quotient within |delta| of a rounding midpoint)
        bound against the reference: 1 ulp
